WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:07.260
Zack Snyder's Justice League is an ambitious
four-hour epic packed with tons of previously

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.440
unseen footage, as well as contradictions
and unexplained moments.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.540
Here are those confusing moments explained.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:20.320
Of all the minor continuity discrepancies
between the DCEU movies, one of the most noticeable

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.780
is the case of Aquaman's eye color.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:28.140
If you missed out on the Whedon cut of Justice
League back in 2017, then you may be used

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.710
to the brown yellows closer to Jason Momoa's
natural eye color in James Wan's Aquaman.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:39.580
In the 2017 Justice League, Aquaman can be
seen with striking baby blues.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.700
This coloring was brought back again in the
Snyder Cut, leaving some viewers reasonably

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.700
confused.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:50.480
As it turns out, this is a choice both directors
are very aware of and cite as an artistic

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.480
difference.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.880
But according to Momoa it was a decision motivated
by something the actor himself said.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:01.050
In an interview with the Huffington Post promoting
Aquaman back in 2018, Momoa revealed about

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.050
the change,

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.780
"It was my idea I brought to Zack.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.670
I thought it would be rad to have a different
eye color.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:11.630
Something that's completely Atlantean that
was, like, his mark...I felt like it was just

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:15.490
something that would be a little otherworldly,
so Zack really liked it."

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.240
As for why director James Wan went in a different
direction, Momoa said,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.729
"I feel like [Wan] wanted to have a warmer
thing in this [movie].

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:28.259
I feel like my justification for [the change]
is that he kind of went home.

00:01:28.259 --> 00:01:31.790
He's more rooted, and he's calm when he's
around his family."

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:38.250
Another inconsistency between Aquaman, Batman
v Superman: Dawn of Justice and Zack Snyder's

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:43.070
Justice League has no real bearing on their
overall plots, but is a detail that can be

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:47.540
irksome to anyone following the DCEU releases
thus far.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.079
It's time to talk about the trident.

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:53.210
In Zack Snyder's Justice League, Queen Atlanna's
[at-LAN-nah] trident is presented to Aquaman

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:54.340
by Vulko.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.850
Vulko encourages Aquaman to take the trident
and claim his birthright to the throne of

00:01:58.850 --> 00:01:59.850
Atlantis.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.060
He declines, setting up the 2018 Aquaman arc.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.579
In the theatrical version of the film, this
was replaced by a scene between Mera [MEER-ah]

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:11.830
and Aquaman after Steppenwolf steals the Mother
Box hidden in Atlantis.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:16.830
It's implied that Mera gives Aquaman the trident
that he uses to fight in the rest of the film.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:21.630
In both cuts of the film, he uses the trident
during battle with the parademons, but the

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:26.540
moment where Aquaman seems to ask for the
trident doesn't exist in the Snyder cut:

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.780
"Then I'm gonna need something from you."

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:35.340
However, that still doesn't explain the footage
first shown in Snyder's previous movie, Batman

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.670
v Superman, which shows Aquaman with a trident
that looks pretty much exactly like the weapon

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.430
he wields in Justice League.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:46.600
Does Aquaman just have an extra trident that
looks just like his mother's?

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.489
This is another perplexing moment that is
a result of creative changes made following

00:02:51.489 --> 00:02:54.239
the lackluster theatrical release of Justice
League.

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:58.959
The way Snyder envisioned underwater communications
between Atlanteans works just fine for his

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:03.050
movie, but James Wan made some changes to
fit his vision for Aquaman.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:08.159
In both versions of Justice League, all underwater
conversations between Atlanteans happen in

00:03:08.159 --> 00:03:09.930
air bubbles they create.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:14.340
In James Wan's Aquaman, the Atlanteans no
longer require these bubbles to speak to each

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.620
other underwater.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.170
Why did this happen?

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:21.140
"Whenever you notice something like that,
a wizard did it."

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.070
Well, not really.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.300
Wan said in an Entertainment Weekly interview,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.110
"People are overthinking it.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:28.430
They're just gonna talk!"

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.920
Honestly, it's hard to imagine him having
any other stance.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.090
Snyder's stylish portrayal of Atlantean communication
would have quickly become annoying in a movie

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.740
that takes place primarily in Atlantis.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.510
To many who grew up with the Justice League
comics and cartoons, Green Lantern is a core

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.310
member of the league.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:52.390
Sadly, the colossal bomb that was 2011's Green
Lantern movie with Ryan Reynolds ensured that

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:56.450
DC won't be touching Hal Jordan for a good
while.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.480
But it seems like Snyder has put the wheels
in motion to one day bring Green Lantern back

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:01.480
into the fold.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.810
The climax of the second part of Zack Snyder's
Justice League is a flashback of epic proportions.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:11.620
It depicts the first age of heroes and the
final battle between Darkseid and the humans,

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.160
Atlanteans, and Amazons.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.490
The one Lantern visible, Yalan Gur, was in
the original cut, but in the Snyder Cut he

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:21.260
explicitly dies by Darkseid's hand.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:26.280
The scene also highlights how the ring escaped
and continued the hero's line, which, presumably,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.580
would've been followed up on in a future Justice
League sequel.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.710
Yalan Gur wasn't the only Lantern that made
an appearance in the Snyder Cut.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:39.430
Kilowog, set up to be one of the main characters
in HBO Max's Green Lantern Corps series, appears

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:44.020
dead in Cyborg's vision of the desolate future
timeline — a.k.a. the "Knightmare."

00:04:45.259 --> 00:04:50.720
One of the most surprising and unusual changes
made to the Snyder Cut comes from a character

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.240
who isn't in the movie for a terribly long
amount of screen time.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.470
Still, it's enough to notice how Snyder has
made Mera change her accent... or at least

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.080
it seems that way.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.870
The truth is probably much simpler.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:09.380
In Aquaman and 2017's Justice League, Heard
voiced the Atlantean queen with an accent

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.750
that sounded more or less like her regular
speaking voice.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.870
"My parents fought in the wars.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:16.870
She took me in."

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:18.040
"What a saint."

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:20.470
"You dare speak of Queen Atlanna that way?"

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:24.610
Zack Snyder's Justice League has Heard putting
on a British accent.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:27.260
"My parents died in the wars.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.660
She took me in."

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.460
"What a saint."

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.560
"You dare speak of Queen Atlanna that way?"

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.280
It's likely that the original scenes with
Mera for the movie were already completely

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.310
finished, and were simply added back for the
Snyder Cut.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:46.990
This means that the Mera scenes in 2017's
Justice League are almost certainly reshoots.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:52.040
This choice is minor and in keeping with Snyder's
objective to completely preserve his original

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:53.040
vision for the film.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:59.330
If you are at all confused about the scene
with Lois Lane and a green space alien impersonating

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:02.080
Martha Kent, don't worry, so are we.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.610
Later revealing himself to Bruce Wayne and
to the DC universe at large, Martian Manhunter

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:10.660
a.k.a. J'onn J'onzz [JON JONES] makes his
live action debut in Zack Snyder's Justice

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.610
League in a mysterious scene.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:17.110
At the end of a long conversation between
Lois and Martha about Clark, Martha exits

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:23.919
and, unbeknownst to Lois, shapeshifts first
into Martian Manhunter and then into Secretary

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:26.110
of Defense Calvin Swanwick.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:31.580
In the scene, Martha advises Lois that she
still needs to be part of society, which motivates

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:36.490
her to get out, placing her in the right place
at the right time when Superman is brought

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:37.600
back to life.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:42.990
This moment is pivotal and the knowledge that
Martian Manhunterwas orchestrating events

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.699
in the movie the whole time is mind-blowing.

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:50.820
Martian Manhunter was one of the original
Justice League members and his appearance

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:56.169
in the Snyderverse brings the league's new
total up to that oh-so-important number seven.

00:06:56.169 --> 00:07:01.349
Now that we know what Snyder planned for two
Justice League sequels, we know he was going

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:05.480
to expand the group and bring in Green Lantern
and other DC legends.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:10.259
But now the future of Martian Manhunter in
the DCEU remains uncertain.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:15.780
A very important part of the Snyderverse is
the Knightmare.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:20.670
It's never really explained in either Batman
v Superman: Dawn of Justice or Zack Snyder's

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:24.210
Justice League, nor is it referred to by name.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:29.340
Still, understanding it is vital to understanding
the greater meaning behind many of the new

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.220
additions to the Snyder Cut.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:37.580
The Knightmare is introduced in Batman v Superman,
when Bruce dreams of being in a sandy, washed-out

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:38.580
desert.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.780
After being ambushed by a group of men, Batman
sees parademons coming in the distance and

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.099
is overtaken.

00:07:44.099 --> 00:07:49.540
This vision concludes with the reveal of an
evil Superman and Bruce being awoken to a

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:53.050
Flash from the future telling him, "Lois is
the key."

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:58.169
It's a reference to what happens in Justice
League, but also is a clue to her importance

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:01.070
in the now shuttered sequels Snyder was planning.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:05.530
The Knightmare itself makes a return in the
Snyder Cut, where it becomes clear that Darkseid

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:08.230
is the cause of the apocalyptic future.

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:13.120
It's never explicitly stated, but Snyder has
explained that this is a vision of the future

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.080
that must be stopped.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:19.849
In the Snyder Cut, we see Superman holding
the burned body of Lois in the Batcave, which

00:08:19.849 --> 00:08:23.410
is the turning point that Bruce must never
let happen.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:28.120
There is a lot to love about Zack Snyder's
Justice League, but admittedly it doesn't

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:32.919
explain some of its biggest concepts — such
as what exactly is the anti-life equation

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:36.240
and why does Darkseid want it so badly?

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.140
Originally created for DC's Fourth World arc
in 1970, the anti-life equation is a secret

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.849
way of controlling all sentient beings.

00:08:43.849 --> 00:08:47.510
Darkseid believes part of the equation exists
in the subconscious of humanity, which is

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:51.470
why Steppenwolf interrogates people using
those creepy spider bugs.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:55.790
Thematically, a common interpretation is that
the equation is a mathematical proof of the

00:08:55.790 --> 00:09:00.540
futility of living and a way to dominate humanity
through spreading hopelessness.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:05.600
What it actually is, and whether or not it
has a physical form in the Snyderverse, is

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.660
one of the biggest mysteries for the future
of the DCEU.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:16.040
It's not explained at all in the movie, but
Superman's pitch black suit has great historical

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:21.740
significance that references one of Snyder's
key inspirations for his Justice League.

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:27.480
The black suit in Zack Snyder's Justice League
is a reference to 1993's The Reign of Superman

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:32.640
comic arc; the follow-up to previous year's
The Death of Superman, which saw Superman

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.560
die while fighting Doomsday.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.380
When he comes back to life in Reign, he's
wearing the black suit in an iconic moment

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:41.529
of DC Comics history.

00:09:41.529 --> 00:09:47.010
Since then, it has always been associated
with the death and return of Superman.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:51.700
In the lore, the suit was designed to help
the weakened Superman absorb more solar energy

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.010
so he can be stronger after coming back to
life.

00:09:54.010 --> 00:10:00.260
While this may be obvious to older DC Comics
fans, it's not left unexplained for no reason.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:04.279
There was actually a deleted scene that was
shot during Snyder's original production that

00:10:04.279 --> 00:10:10.430
showed a newly resurrected Superman choosing
to don either a suit of Kryptonian armor or

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:12.160
the iconic black suit.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:16.660
Joe Manganiello joined the DC universe as
Deathstroke in a mid-credits scene in the

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.899
theatrical cut of Justice League back in 2017.

00:10:19.899 --> 00:10:25.970
The yacht scene that served as his introduction
is in the Snyder Cut, but in an altered form.

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:30.350
However, the restored version of the scene
is more confusing than anything else.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:34.510
In the theatrical Justice League, Deathstroke's
introduction was a teaser for a Legion of

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:39.160
Doom movie where Luthor gathers a band of
villains to face off against the Justice League.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:43.740
"Shouldn't we have a league of our own?"

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:48.250
In the version of the scene that made it into
the Snyder Cut, Luthor tells Deathstroke Batman's

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:51.720
true identity, which is a significant difference.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:56.940
At the time, Snyder's intention was to make
a standalone Ben Affleck Batman film following

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:00.839
Justice League, with Manganiello's Deathstroke
serving as the main villain.

00:11:00.839 --> 00:11:06.029
Affleck dropped out and The Batman eventually
turned into the Robert Pattinson-led, Matt

00:11:06.029 --> 00:11:08.440
Reeves-directed project currently in production.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:13.250
Now, both versions of Justice League have
scenes teasing movies that will never see

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:14.250
the light of day.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:20.510
The Knightmare scene in the epilogue is the
most dire of all.

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:24.430
Parademons roam the land while Batman and
the rest of the League are at the end of their

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:25.430
rope.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:30.410
Aquaman is dead, replaced by Mera, Deathstroke
is working with the League, and Flash is wearing

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:34.220
his future costume from the BvS flashback.

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:38.560
Jared Leto's Joker makes his return to mock
the Dark Knight one last time.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:44.010
He and Batman make important references to
the deaths of Robin, Harley Quinn, and Lois

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:47.800
Lane before Joker offers Bruce a truce.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:52.959
The sequence ends with the League preparing
for what might just be one last battle at

00:11:52.959 --> 00:11:55.410
the end of the world with Superman.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:59.250
The scene is loaded with implications about
what might have happened in the future of

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:00.269
the Snyderverse.

00:12:00.269 --> 00:12:04.610
It confirms the death of Robin at the hands
of the Joker and that Bruce letting Lois die

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:09.329
in a tragic blast is the turning point of
the conflict according to Snyder.

00:12:09.329 --> 00:12:13.800
This tragedy allows Superman to succumb to
the anti-life equation and therefore fall

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.660
completely under Darkseid's control.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:20.600
Time travel and multiverses were going to
play a major role in Justice League 2 and

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:27.230
3, but now this single scene is the most we'll
see of Zack Snyder's dark Justice League trilogy.

